WEBVTT

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:04.890
Preflight checks should verify aircraft condition, batteries, controller, firmware state,

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:09.315
storage, payload, lighting, home point, navigation status, signal, return settings,

00:00:09.315 --> 00:00:11.710
weather, site control, and crew communications.

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:14.015
Conduct a final darkness-specific hazard review.

00:00:14.015 --> 00:00:15.160
Use a deliberate takeoff.

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:19.565
Establish stable control and confirm orientation before departing the recovery area.

00:00:19.565 --> 00:00:23.360
Keep the initial route simple and maintain altitude and clearance appropriate to the

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:24.880
approved plan and site hazards.

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:29.160
During flight, monitor aircraft lights, visual position, telemetry, battery, signal,

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:32.095
navigation behavior, weather, people, and obstacles.

00:00:32.095 --> 00:00:35.575
Payload work should pause whenever the pilot needs attention for basic control or

00:00:35.575 --> 00:00:37.006
situational awareness.

00:00:37.006 --> 00:00:40.765
Return-to-home depends on correct home point, navigation, route, altitude assumptions,

00:00:40.765 --> 00:00:43.320
obstacles, and an available recovery area.

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:46.590
Review those assumptions before launch and remain prepared for manual intervention

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:48.560
according to training and procedures.

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:51.840
If orientation is lost, avoid rapid speculative inputs.

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:55.750
Stabilize if possible, communicate, use verified telemetry and visual cues,

00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:58.440
and execute the planned return or landing action.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:02.405
If signal degrades, do not continue farther because control has not yet been lost.

00:01:02.405 --> 00:01:05.660
Abort criteria must trigger early enough to preserve options.

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:09.450
Lighting failure, unexpected obstacles, weak signal, navigation uncertainty,

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:12.130
falling visibility, battery anomaly, crew fatigue,

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:14.890
or privacy concerns can all justify ending the mission.
